            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Sets every value within lambda of zero to exactly 0, and passes everything outside that band unchanged.\n\nIt is a dead zone, or a hard noise gate on values: small wobbles around zero disappear, and anything big enough survives at full size. Use it to ignore jitter in a control that rests near zero, or to keep only the large coefficients of a transform.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nFlat at 0 from -lambda to lambda, then the input itself outside. At the edges of the band the output JUMPS from 0 straight to plus or minus lambda - a value just past the threshold comes out at full size. Lambda 0 passes everything. (A negative lambda is accepted and also passes everything.)\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nn.hardshrink\nNo arguments; default lambda 0.5.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to bend. Receiving it triggers the node. A list, number or numpy array is turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu first; a tensor arriving as a tensor keeps its own dtype and device. The curve is applied to every element on its own, so the output has the same shape as the input. An INTEGER tensor (t.arange with whole numbers makes one) is refused: the console reports \"not implemented for 'Long'\" and nothing is sent. Put a t.to float32 in front of it.\n\nlambda (drag float on the node, and an inlet):\nThe half-width of the dead zone. Default 0.5. It does not trigger the node: the next tensor uses it. In the demo the metro re-sends the ramp ten times a second, so the curve follows the slider.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe bent tensor, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.linspace makes 128 values running evenly from -4 (left edge of the plot) to 4 (right edge); the middle of the plot is 0. The plot draws two lines over each other: the straight diagonal is the input itself, and the other is the node's output for the same values. Where the two lines coincide the node passes the value unchanged; where they part, that is the bend. The plot's height runs from -4 to 4; the input line runs off the top and bottom where it leaves that range.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.softshrink has the same dead zone without the jump: survivors are pulled towards zero by lambda. t.nn.Threshold zeroes (or replaces) only the values below a level, on one side. t.nn.tanhshrink is a dead zone with no corners at all. noise_gate does this to a stream of numbers, with smoothing.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 780,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 855,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Sets every value within lambda of zero to exactly 0, and passes everything outside that band unchanged.\n\nIt is a dead zone, or a hard noise gate on values: small wobbles around zero disappear, and anything big enough survives at full size. Use it to ignore jitter in a control that rests near zero, or to keep only the large coefficients of a transform.\n\nTHE CURVE:\nFlat at 0 from -lambda to lambda, then the input itself outside. At the edges of the band the output JUMPS from 0 straight to plus or minus lambda - a value just past the threshold comes out at full size. Lambda 0 passes everything. (A negative lambda is accepted and also passes everything.)\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.nn.hardshrink\nNo arguments; default lambda 0.5.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor in:\nThe tensor to bend. Receiving it triggers the node. A list, number or numpy array is turned into a float32 tensor on the cpu first; a tensor arriving as a tensor keeps its own dtype and device. The curve is applied to every element on its own, so the output has the same shape as the input. An INTEGER tensor (t.arange with whole numbers makes one) is refused: the console reports \"not implemented for 'Long'\" and nothing is sent. Put a t.to float32 in front of it.\n\nlambda (drag float on the node, and an inlet):\nThe half-width of the dead zone. Default 0.5. It does not trigger the node: the next tensor uses it. In the demo the metro re-sends the ramp ten times a second, so the curve follows the slider.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\noutput:\nThe bent tensor, the same shape as the input.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nt.linspace makes 128 values running evenly from -4 (left edge of the plot) to 4 (right edge); the middle of the plot is 0. The plot draws two lines over each other: the straight diagonal is the input itself, and the other is the node's output for the same values. Where the two lines coincide the node passes the value unchanged; where they part, that is the bend. The plot's height runs from -4 to 4; the input line runs off the top and bottom where it leaves that range.\n\nRELATED:\nt.nn.softshrink has the same dead zone without the jump: survivors are pulled towards zero by lambda. t.nn.Threshold zeroes (or replaces) only the values below a level, on one side. t.nn.tanhshrink is a dead zone with no corners at all. noise_gate does this to a stream of numbers, with smoothing."
        },
